==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|deflecto}}, from {{m|la|de-||away}} + {{m|la|flecto||to bend}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dɪˈflɛkt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkt}}
{{rfap|en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make]] (something) [[deviate]] from its original [[path]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|ball games}} To [[touch]] the [[ball]], often [[unwittingly]], after a [[shot]] or a sharp [[pass]], thereby making it [[unpredictable]] for the other players.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[deviate]] from its original [[path]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[avoid]] [[address]]ing ''(questions, criticism, etc.).''
#: {{syn|en|elude}}
#: ''The Prime Minister '''deflected''' some increasingly pointed questions by claiming he had an appointment.''
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[divert]] ''(attention, etc.).''
#* '''2013''', Luke Harding and Uki Goni, ''Argentina urges UK to hand back Falklands and 'end colonialism'' (in ''The Guardian'', 3 January 2013)[http://www.guardian.co.uk/uk/2013/jan/02/argentina-britain-hand-back-falklands]
#*: Critics suggest that Fernández, an unashamed populist and nationalist, is seeking to '''deflect''' attention from social disharmony at home.

